# The Celerrate refinements overlay (design section 7): enriched
# stub signatures in the internal norm, compiled into stubs.bin by
# `cargo xtask compile-stubs`. Validated at compile time (existence)
# and by the lowering-totality test in celerrate_types (grammar).
#
# Curation is measurement-driven (plan 7, task 11): an entry earns its
# place only when it both names a callee the pinned corpus exercises and
# demonstrably sharpens a measured result (the ground-truth or mixed-rate
# baseline). array_slice and array_unique carry the element type through
# to array_map/array_filter, resolving real precision-gap records;
# inert-but-in-table returns (count, implode, in_array) moved no
# instrument and only duplicate the base fold, so they stay out.
#
# Task-12 debt (owner: this overlay).
#
# - By-reference stdlib mutators (array_shift, usort, sort, and their
#   family) carry no refinement here and are not claimed by
#   StdlibProvider's by-reference channel either: they stay on
#   whatever write-back the base phpstorm-stubs declared signature
#   already gives their by-reference parameter. Curation revisits only
#   if the corpus's per-callee mixed-rate table ever demands it —
#   unmeasured today, not by omission.
# - Decision-15 top-ten exit, named exceptions (task 11's measured
#   exit, verbatim): array_pop stays mixed (rank 10, mixed 3/3) —
#   caller opacity, its sole corpus call site feeds it an untyped
#   `array` (element type `mixed`) from an untyped method return, and
#   no signature on array_pop itself can fix a caller's opacity.
#   max (mixed 2/2) and min (mixed 1/1), both below the top ten: the
#   return is the join of the argument types, computation-dependent
#   and provider territory, not a declarative refinement — combined
#   demand of 3 calls, not a genuine case for a new provider handler.

function array_keys<TKey, TValue>(array<TKey, TValue> $array): list<TKey>
function array_slice<TKey, TValue>(array<TKey, TValue> $array): array<TKey, TValue>
function array_unique<TKey, TValue>(array<TKey, TValue> $array): array<TKey, TValue>
function array_values<TKey, TValue>(array<TKey, TValue> $array): list<TValue>

class ArrayIterator<TKey, TValue> implements Iterator<TKey, TValue> {
    method __construct(array<TKey, TValue> $array)
    method current(): TValue
    method key(): TKey
}
